c :: foreign { printf : fn (*char, ...) u0 }

// BUG: decimal integer literals in the valid u64 range [2^63, 2^64 - 1] are
// rejected by the lexer with a bogus "Invalid integer range" error.
//
// Repro:
//   zinc tests/bugs/03_u64_literal_above_i64max_rejected.zn -o /tmp/out
// Observed:
//   error: Invalid integer range 9223372036
//   (and for 18446744073709551615: "Invalid integer range 1844674407")
//
// Expected: this is a perfectly valid u64 value and should compile, printing
//   9223372036854775808
//
// Root cause:
//   src/zlex.c parseNumber() lexes every decimal literal with strtoll() (signed
//   64-bit), whose maximum is 2^63 - 1 = 9223372036854775807. Any literal from
//   2^63 up to the u64 max sets errno == ERANGE and is reported as invalid, even
//   though it fits in u64. (Hex/binary literals use strtoull and do not hit this,
//   though they are then truncated by the separate i32 codegen bug — see bug 01.)
//   The error text also prints the overflowed accumulator ("9223372036") rather
//   than the source literal.
main :: fn () i32 {
    x: u64 = 9223372036854775808
    c::printf("%llu\n", x)
    return 0
}
